    {
      "id": "adr-adr-repo",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Decision records as files in the repository",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 0,
      "concern": "code-documentation",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code lives in this element and will be read, changed and operated by people who were not there when it was written, and by the same people a year later. The forces: a decision that is not written down is re-argued at every change, or quietly reversed; documentation kept away from the code is not updated when the code is, and a page that was true once reads as if it still were; generated reference is always current but says what, never why; a wiki reaches readers who never open a repository, at the price of living where no review and no build can check it; a diagram drawn once drifts from the system it shows the moment the next pull request merges; and every page costs time to keep, so the set has to be small enough that it is kept.",
      "decision": "Every architecturally significant decision is one Markdown file in {{ADR_DIR}}, numbered and named NNNN-title.md, in the MADR template (YAML front matter with status, date, deciders; sections Context and Problem Statement, Considered Options, Decision Outcome, Consequences), created with adr-tools or log4brains and recorded within {{ADR_DAYS}} days. The record is changed in the pull request of the first code that implements it, reviewed by the same reviewers; a superseding record sets the old one's status to superseded with a link both ways, and old records are never rewritten. A test over the front matter runs in CI. API reference: {{API_DOCS}}. READMEs: {{READMES}}. Documentation: {{DOCS_SITE}}. Diagrams: {{DIAGRAMS}}.",
      "consequences": "• Decisions are versioned, reviewed and searched like code, and travel with the repository when it is forked or archived.\n• Readers who do not open the repository (product, operations, auditors) need a published copy: log4brains or the docs site renders the folder.\n• The template guarantees fields, not quality: a record with a one-line context passes the lint and still explains nothing; reviewers have to read it.\n• Decisions spanning several repositories live in one of them or in a shared one, and the others link to it.",
      "alternatives": "1. Decisions in an architecture model next to the code: links each decision to the elements it constrains and lets CI check them together, but needs the model to exist and be kept.\n2. A decision log in a wiki: reaches readers outside the repository, but no review with the code and nothing checks it.\n3. Decisions in commit messages or pull request descriptions: already written, but unsearchable by topic and lost when the branch is squashed.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DECISIONS": [
          "adr-repo"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-model",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Decisions in the architecture model next to the code",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 130,
      "concern": "code-documentation",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code lives in this element and will be read, changed and operated by people who were not there when it was written, and by the same people a year later. The forces: a decision that is not written down is re-argued at every change, or quietly reversed; documentation kept away from the code is not updated when the code is, and a page that was true once reads as if it still were; generated reference is always current but says what, never why; a wiki reaches readers who never open a repository, at the price of living where no review and no build can check it; a diagram drawn once drifts from the system it shows the moment the next pull request merges; and every page costs time to keep, so the set has to be small enough that it is kept.",
      "decision": "Decisions are elements of the architecture model kept in {{MODEL_DIR}} in the repository: with radical.tools, ADR nodes in the Markdown folder with status, date, context, decision, consequences and alternatives, linked with constrains to the containers and components they bind, with derives to the requirements behind them and with implements from the fitness functions that enforce them, and `radical check {{MODEL_DIR}}` runs in CI and fails on a decision without status or a constrained element; with Structurizr, ADRs in the workspace's decisions folder shown in its ADR view. A decision is recorded within {{ADR_DAYS}} days and changed in the same pull request as the first code that implements it; a superseding decision links the old one and leaves it in place. API reference: {{API_DOCS}}. READMEs: {{READMES}}. Documentation: {{DOCS_SITE}}. Diagrams: {{DIAGRAMS}}.",
      "consequences": "• A reader of an element reaches its decisions, requirements and checks from the element, and a decision without a subject is an error, not a forgotten file.\n• The model has to be kept: a team that stops updating it has stale decisions and stale diagrams at once.\n• The model folder is reviewable as text, but the tool is one more thing to install for reading and editing (CLI, Studio or an editor for the DSL).\n• Decisions are published as part of the model, not as a page on their own; readers outside the repository need the rendered site or a viewer.",
      "alternatives": "1. Decision records as plain files in {{ADR_DIR}}: no tool to learn, but the link from a decision to what it constrains is prose and nothing checks it.\n2. A decision log in a wiki: reaches readers outside the repository, but no review with the code and nothing checks it.\n3. Decisions both in the model and in a wiki: two copies drift; keep one and link to it.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DECISIONS": [
          "model"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-wiki",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "A decision log in a wiki or document space",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 260,
      "concern": "code-documentation",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code lives in this element and will be read, changed and operated by people who were not there when it was written, and by the same people a year later. The forces: a decision that is not written down is re-argued at every change, or quietly reversed; documentation kept away from the code is not updated when the code is, and a page that was true once reads as if it still were; generated reference is always current but says what, never why; a wiki reaches readers who never open a repository, at the price of living where no review and no build can check it; a diagram drawn once drifts from the system it shows the moment the next pull request merges; and every page costs time to keep, so the set has to be small enough that it is kept. Here most readers never open the repository: decisions have to be found, read and commented on in the space where those readers already work.",
      "decision": "Decisions are pages in the team's space (Confluence with the Decision template, or a Notion database with the same fields): title, status (proposed, accepted, superseded), date, deciders, context, decision, consequences, options considered. One decision log index page per system lists every decision with status and date; the repository README and the module implementing a decision link to its page. A decision is recorded within {{ADR_DAYS}} days; superseding sets the old page's status and links both ways, and old pages are not edited. A scheduled script over the space's REST API reports pages missing a status or date and decisions not on the index. API reference: {{API_DOCS}}. READMEs: {{READMES}}. Documentation: {{DOCS_SITE}}. Diagrams: {{DIAGRAMS}}.",
      "consequences": "• Product, operations and an architecture board read and comment where they already are, with the space's search and notifications.\n• The decision is not reviewed with the code: the reviewer of the pull request has to open the page, and a code change that contradicts a decision is caught by people, not by a check.\n• The space is outside version control: history is the page history, and an export is needed when the space is moved or shut.\n• Links from code to pages break when pages move; the script catches missing pages, not moved ones.",
      "alternatives": "1. Decision records as files in {{ADR_DIR}}: reviewed with the code and versioned, but invisible to readers who do not open repositories unless published.\n2. Decisions in an architecture model next to the code: connected and checked, but the furthest from readers who live in the wiki.\n3. Files in the repository published to the wiki by a job: one source and both audiences, at the cost of a job to keep and comments that land on the copy.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DECISIONS": [
          "wiki"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-adr-lint",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Decision records are well-formed",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 0,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "Every file in {{ADR_DIR}} has status, date and title; every superseded record links to a record that exists; 0 records in status proposed older than {{ADR_DAYS}} days",
      "description": "A test reads the MADR front matter of every record in {{ADR_DIR}}, checks the required fields and that each superseded-by link points at an existing record, and fails on a record left in proposed past the deadline. log4brains or adr-tools can create the files; this test keeps them honest.\n\nTool: Vitest with gray-matter (a test over the ADR folder)\n\n```ts\nimport { readdirSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport matter from 'gray-matter'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\n\nconst DIR = 'docs/adr'   // ADR_DIR\nconst DAYS = 14          // ADR_DAYS\nconst files = readdirSync(DIR).filter((f) => /^\\d{4}-.+\\.md$/.test(f))\n\nit.each(files)('%s is a well-formed decision record', (file) => {\n  const { data } = matter(readFileSync(`${DIR}/${file}`, 'utf8'))\n  expect(['proposed', 'accepted', 'superseded', 'deprecated']).toContain(data.status)\n  expect(data.date).toMatch(/^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$/)\n  if (data.status === 'superseded') expect(files).toContain(String(data['superseded-by']))\n  if (data.status === 'proposed') expect((Date.now() - Date.parse(data.date)) / 86_400_000).toBeLessThanOrEqual(DAYS)\n})\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DECISIONS": [
          "adr-repo"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-radical-check",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Architecture model checked in CI",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 130,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "`radical check {{MODEL_DIR}}` reports 0 errors on every pull request: no decision without status, date or a constrained element, no broken link, no gap above its baseline",
      "description": "The CI job runs the model check against the test reports of the same run, so a decision without a subject, a fitness function naming a test that did not run, or a link to a deleted element fails the pull request. With Structurizr, `structurizr-cli validate` on the workspace plays the same role for the DSL.\n\nTool: radical CLI in GitHub Actions\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/architecture.yml\nname: architecture model\non: [pull_request, push]\njobs:\n  check:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 5\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with: { node-version-file: .nvmrc, cache: npm }\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm test -- --reporter=junit --outputFile=reports/junit.xml\n      - run: npx radical check architecture --reports reports   # MODEL_DIR; rules and baselines in architecture/check.json\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DECISIONS": [
          "model"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-wiki-log",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Decision log complete in the wiki",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 260,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 decision pages without a status and a date; 0 decisions missing from the index page; 0 pages in status proposed older than {{ADR_DAYS}} days",
      "description": "A weekly job lists the pages of the decision space through the Confluence REST API, reads the status and date properties, compares the list with the links on the index page, and posts the gaps to the team channel; the job fails when any gap is older than a week.\n\nTool: Confluence REST API with curl and jq (scheduled job)\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# weekly: decision pages with their status and date properties\nset -euo pipefail\nSPACE=ARCH; DAYS=14   # ADR_DAYS\ncurl -sf -u \"$CONFLUENCE_USER:$CONFLUENCE_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"$CONFLUENCE_URL/wiki/rest/api/content/search?cql=space=$SPACE+and+label=decision&expand=metadata.properties.status,metadata.properties.date&limit=200\" \\\n  | jq -r '.results[] | [.id, .title, .metadata.properties.status.value // \"MISSING\", .metadata.properties.date.value // \"MISSING\"] | @tsv' > decisions.tsv\nawk -F'\\t' '$3==\"MISSING\" || $4==\"MISSING\" { print \"no status or date: \" $2; bad=1 } END { exit bad }' decisions.tsv\nawk -F'\\t' -v days=$DAYS 'BEGIN { now=systime() } $3==\"proposed\" && (now - mktime(gensub(/-/, \" \", \"g\", $4) \" 0 0 0\")) / 86400 > days { print \"proposed too long: \" $2; bad=1 } END { exit bad }' decisions.tsv\n# every decision page is linked from the index page\nINDEX=$(curl -sf -u \"$CONFLUENCE_USER:$CONFLUENCE_TOKEN\" \"$CONFLUENCE_URL/wiki/rest/api/content/$INDEX_PAGE_ID?expand=body.storage\" | jq -r .body.storage.value)\ncut -f1 decisions.tsv | while read -r id; do grep -q \"pages/$id\" <<< \"$INDEX\" || { echo \"not on the index: $id\"; exit 1; }; done\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DECISIONS": [
          "wiki"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-readme-sections",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Every module has its README sections",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 390,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "Every workspace or module directory holds README.md with the headings {{README_SECTIONS}}; 0 directories without",
      "description": "A test lists the module directories from the workspace configuration (package.json workspaces, or the services folder), not from a list kept by hand, and expects a README.md with every required heading in each; a new module fails the test until it has one.\n\nTool: Vitest (a test over the workspaces)\n\n```ts\nimport { existsSync, globSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\n\nconst SECTIONS = ['Purpose', 'Run', 'Test', 'Owner']   // README_SECTIONS\nconst workspaces: string[] = JSON.parse(readFileSync('package.json', 'utf8')).workspaces\nconst dirs = workspaces.flatMap((w) => globSync(w))\n\nit.each(dirs)('%s has a README with the required sections', (dir) => {\n  const file = `${dir}/README.md`\n  expect(existsSync(file), `${file} is missing`).toBe(true)\n  const headings = readFileSync(file, 'utf8').match(/^#{1,3} .+$/gm) ?? []\n  for (const s of SECTIONS) expect(headings.some((h) => h.toLowerCase().includes(s.toLowerCase())), `${file} lacks \"${s}\"`).toBe(true)\n})\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "READMES": [
          "per-module"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-readme-table",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Root README lists every module",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 520,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "Every module directory has a row in the root README's module table with purpose and owner; 0 rows naming a directory that no longer exists",
      "description": "A test reads the module table of the root README and compares it with the module directories of the repository both ways: a directory without a row and a row without a directory each fail it.\n\nTool: Vitest (a test over README.md)\n\n```ts\nimport { readdirSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\n\nconst readme = readFileSync('README.md', 'utf8')\nconst rows = [...readme.matchAll(/^\\| `?(src\\/[\\w-]+)`? \\| (.+?) \\| (.+?) \\|$/gm)]   // | dir | purpose | owner |\nconst listed = new Map(rows.map((r) => [r[1], { purpose: r[2].trim(), owner: r[3].trim() }]))\nconst dirs = readdirSync('src', { withFileTypes: true }).filter((d) => d.isDirectory()).map((d) => `src/${d.name}`)\n\nit('every module is in the table, with purpose and owner', () => {\n  expect(dirs.filter((d) => !listed.has(d))).toEqual([])\n  for (const [dir, row] of listed) expect(row.purpose && row.owner, `${dir} lacks purpose or owner`).toBeTruthy()\n})\nit('no row names a module that is gone', () => {\n  expect([...listed.keys()].filter((d) => !dirs.includes(d))).toEqual([])\n})\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "READMES": [
          "root-only"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-doc-comments",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Doc comments on the public API",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 650,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "At least {{DOC_COMMENT_COVERAGE_PCT}}% of exported symbols carry a doc comment with a summary; a missing comment on a new export is a lint error",
      "description": "eslint-plugin-jsdoc requires a doc comment on every exported function, class and type, as an error on new code; for Python, interrogate measures docstring coverage and fails under the threshold (`interrogate --fail-under {{DOC_COMMENT_COVERAGE_PCT}} src`); for Java, the javadoc doclint `-Xdoclint:all` run in the build.\n\nTool: eslint-plugin-jsdoc (or interrogate for Python)\n\n```js\n// eslint.config.js\nimport jsdoc from 'eslint-plugin-jsdoc'\nexport default [\n  jsdoc.configs['flat/recommended-typescript-error'],\n  {\n    files: ['src/**/*.ts'],\n    rules: {\n      'jsdoc/require-jsdoc': ['error', {\n        publicOnly: true,                       // exported symbols only\n        require: { FunctionDeclaration: true, ClassDeclaration: true, MethodDefinition: true },\n        contexts: ['TSInterfaceDeclaration', 'TSTypeAliasDeclaration', 'ExportNamedDeclaration > VariableDeclaration'],\n      }],\n      'jsdoc/require-description': 'error',\n      'jsdoc/require-param-description': 'error',\n    },\n  },\n]\n// Python: interrogate --fail-under 90 src   # DOC_COMMENT_COVERAGE_PCT\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "API_DOCS": [
          "generated",
          "written"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-reference-build",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "API reference builds without warnings",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 780,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "The reference builds on every pull request with 0 warnings (an unresolved link or an undocumented export is a warning) and is published with each release",
      "description": "TypeDoc runs with warnings treated as errors, so a broken `{@link}` or an export without a comment fails the pull request; Sphinx does the same with `sphinx-build -W --keep-going`. The release job publishes the output under the release version.\n\nTool: TypeDoc (or Sphinx)\n\n```json\n// typedoc.json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://typedoc.org/schema.json\",\n  \"entryPoints\": [\"src/index.ts\"],\n  \"out\": \"docs/api\",\n  \"treatWarningsAsErrors\": true,\n  \"validation\": { \"notExported\": true, \"invalidLink\": true, \"notDocumented\": true },\n  \"requiredToBeDocumented\": [\"Function\", \"Class\", \"Interface\", \"TypeAlias\", \"Method\"],\n  \"includeVersion\": true\n}\n// package.json: \"docs:api\": \"typedoc\"   (CI: npm run docs:api on every pull request)\n// Python: sphinx-build -W --keep-going -b html docs docs/_build\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "API_DOCS": [
          "generated"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-contract-drift",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Contract and code agree",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 910,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 differences between the published OpenAPI contract and the specification generated from the running code, on every pull request",
      "description": "The pipeline starts the element, fetches the specification it generates from its routes and compares it with the contract file in the repository with oasdiff; any difference fails the build, so the contract, the generated reference and the code cannot drift apart. Whether a difference may be released at all is the API evolution concern's decision; this check only keeps the documentation true.\n\nTool: oasdiff\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# CI step: the contract in the repository is what the code serves\nset -euo pipefail\nnpm start & PID=$!\nuntil curl -sf http://localhost:3000/openapi.json > generated.json; do sleep 1; done\nkill $PID\n# any difference in paths, parameters, schemas or responses fails\noasdiff diff openapi.yaml generated.json --fail-on-diff --exclude-elements description,examples,title\n# and a breaking change is reported on its own for the API evolution review\noasdiff breaking openapi.yaml generated.json --fail-on ERR\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "API_DOCS": [
          "generated"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-doctest",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Examples in the guide run",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1040,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "Every code example in the API guide is executed on each pull request; 0 examples failing or skipped",
      "description": "markdown-doctest executes every fenced JavaScript and TypeScript block of the guide with the package imported, failing on an exception; for Python, `pytest --doctest-modules --doctest-glob=\"docs/*.md\"` runs the doctests in docstrings and Markdown. Examples that need a server are marked and run against a test instance instead of being skipped.\n\nTool: markdown-doctest (or pytest --doctest-modules)\n\n```js\n// .markdown-doctest-setup.js\nconst sdk = require('./dist')\nmodule.exports = {\n  require: { '@shop/sdk': sdk },                      // what the examples import\n  globals: { client: sdk.createClient({ baseUrl: process.env.API_URL }) },\n  regexRequire: { '^@shop/sdk/(.+)$': (m, sub) => require(`./dist/${sub}`) },\n  ignore: ['**/CHANGELOG.md'],\n}\n// package.json: \"test:docs\": \"markdown-doctest docs/api/*.md README.md\"\n// Python: pytest --doctest-modules --doctest-glob=\"docs/*.md\" src docs\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "API_DOCS": [
          "written"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-docs-build",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Docs build strictly with links checked",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1170,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "The documentation builds in strict mode with 0 warnings and lychee finds 0 broken links, within {{LINK_CHECK_MINUTES}} minutes, on every pull request",
      "description": "The docs job builds the site with warnings as errors (`mkdocs build --strict`, or Docusaurus with onBrokenLinks: \"throw\") and runs lychee over the Markdown and the built HTML; the job has a timeout of {{LINK_CHECK_MINUTES}} minutes and is a required check.\n\nTool: MkDocs (--strict) with lychee in GitHub Actions\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/docs.yml\nname: docs\non: [pull_request]\njobs:\n  docs:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 5   # LINK_CHECK_MINUTES\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with: { python-version: '3.12' }\n      - run: pip install mkdocs-material && mkdocs build --strict   # Docusaurus: onBrokenLinks: 'throw'\n      - uses: lycheeverse/lychee-action@v2\n        with:\n          args: --no-progress --include-fragments --exclude-path site/assets 'docs/**/*.md' 'site/**/*.html'\n          fail: true\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DOCS_SITE": [
          "docs-as-code"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-stale-pages",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "No page without owner or past review",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1300,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "Every page has an owner and a review date in its front matter; 0 pages with a review date older than {{MAX_DOC_AGE_DAYS}} days on the main branch",
      "description": "A test over the docs folder reads each page's front matter and fails on a page without an owner or review date; a weekly job runs the same test with the age limit and opens an issue assigned to the owner for each stale page.\n\nTool: Vitest with gray-matter (a test over the docs folder)\n\n```ts\nimport { globSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport matter from 'gray-matter'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\n\nconst MAX_DAYS = Number(process.env.MAX_DOC_AGE_DAYS ?? 365)   // MAX_DOC_AGE_DAYS\nconst pages = globSync('docs/**/*.md')\n\nit.each(pages)('%s has an owner and a recent review date', (page) => {\n  const { data } = matter(readFileSync(page, 'utf8'))   // --- owner: team-orders\\n reviewed: 2026-10-01 ---\n  expect(data.owner, `${page} has no owner`).toBeTruthy()\n  expect(String(data.reviewed)).toMatch(/^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$/)\n  if (process.env.CHECK_AGE) expect((Date.now() - Date.parse(data.reviewed)) / 86_400_000, `${page} is stale`).toBeLessThanOrEqual(MAX_DAYS)\n})\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DOCS_SITE": [
          "docs-as-code"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-diagram-drift",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Structural change touches the model",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1430,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 merged pull requests that add or remove a dependency, service client or data store under a diagrammed module without a change in {{MODEL_DIR}}",
      "description": "A CI step lists the files a pull request changes; when a dependency manifest, a client of another service or a data-store configuration under a diagrammed module changed and nothing under {{MODEL_DIR}} did, the step fails with the list, and the author either updates the model or explains in the pull request why the structure did not change. With Structurizr or PlantUML the diagram source folder takes the model folder's place.\n\nTool: git diff --name-only (CI script)\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# CI: a change to the structure of a diagrammed module changes the model too\nset -euo pipefail\nMODEL=architecture   # MODEL_DIR\nBASE=$(git merge-base origin/main HEAD)\nchanged=$(git diff --name-only \"$BASE\"...HEAD)\n# files that change the structure: dependency manifests, service clients, data-store config\nstructural=$(grep -E '^(apps|packages|services)/[^/]+/(package\\.json|pom\\.xml|go\\.mod|requirements\\.txt|src/(clients|adapters|infra)/.*|src/.*(Client|Repository|Gateway)\\.[a-z]+)$' <<< \"$changed\" || true)\nif [ -n \"$structural\" ] && ! grep -q \"^$MODEL/\" <<< \"$changed\"; then\n  echo \"These files change the structure of a diagrammed module, but $MODEL/ did not change:\"; echo \"$structural\"\n  echo \"Update the model in this pull request (radical check lists what is missing) or explain why the structure is the same.\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DIAGRAMS": [
          "model"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
